contaminant-detection\n\n## Key Improvements Implemented\n\n1. **Domain-Specific Feature Engineering**: Created features based on the physical nature of S-parameter measurements from microwave sensing:\n   - Sum, mean, and variance of all S-parameters \n   - Ratio features between symmetric S-parameter pairs (s12/s21, s13/s31)\n   - Difference features between symmetric S-parameter pairs (s12-s21, s13-s31)\n\n2. **Feature Selection Strategy**: \n   - Focused on the most informative combinations rather than creating excessive features\n   - Limited to ~37 features to maintain model generalization\n   - Used appropriate numerical stability with epsilon for division operations\n\n3. **Physical Insights Applied**:\n   - Leveraged the symmetric nature of S-parameter matrices (sij ≈ sji)\n   - Created meaningful ratios and differences that capture physical relationships\n   - Added statistical summaries that encode the overall behavior of the S-parameters\n\n## Performance Results\n- Baseline (P0): 0.00723 (1-auroc)\n- Best Score Achieved: 0.00702 (1-auroc) \n- Improvement: ~2.7% reduction in error\n\n## Why This Worked\nThe S-parameter data has inherent symmetries and physical relationships that are captured well by the ratio and difference features. The statistical summaries (sum, mean, variance) provide good overall characterization of the signal behavior, while the symmetric pair features exploit the known structure of the data."}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct","usage":{"input":17197,"output":408,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":17605,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1790973441837,"responseId":"chatcmpl-a0d52102aa7b0561","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"off"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_a1985943c1c94585bf6e10b8","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote to NOTES.md"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1790973443963},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my analysis and testing, I have successfully optimized the pipeline for the hazelnut-spread-contaminant-detection dataset. Here's what I accomplished:\n\n## Summary of Improvements\n\nI identified that the key to improving performance was in creating domain-specific features that leverage the physical properties of S-parameter measurements from microwave sensing. My best-performing pipeline (eval #9) achieved a 1-auroc score of 0.00702, which represents a 2.7% improvement over the baseline of 0.00723.\n\n## Key Changes Made\n\n1. **Domain-Specific Feature Engineering**: Created features that take advantage of the symmetric nature of S-parameter matrices:\n   - Sum, mean, and variance of all S-parameters\n   - Ratio features between symmetric pairs (s12/s21, s13/s31)\n   - Difference features between symmetric pairs (s12-s21, s13-s31)\n\n2. **Strategic Feature Selection**: Rather than creating excessive features, I focused on the most informative combinations (~37 features total) to maintain model generalization while capturing important patterns.\n\n## Why This Approach Worked\n\nThe S-parameter data has inherent physical relationships that are crucial for contaminant detection. By creating features that explicitly capture these relationships (symmetric pairs, statistical summaries), the frozen TabPFN model could better distinguish between contaminated and non-contaminated samples.\n\nMy optimized pipeline is now ready and achieves the best score among all tested configurations. The solution is kept minimal and focused on the actual data characteristics rather than complex transformations or overfitting to noise.\n\nThe final pipeline is in `pipeline.py` and the detailed explanation is in `NOTES.md`.\n"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct","usage":{"input":17624,"output":356,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite"